### 🟠 Finding 4 — No Request Body Parsing or Input Validation (OWASP A03)

**Problem:** The app has no `express.json()` middleware and no input validation. When POST/PUT routes are added, unsanitized input will flow directly into database queries, template engines, or business logic — the root cause of SQL injection, NoSQL injection, and XSS.

**Fix:** Enable body parsing with a size cap, and validate/sanitize all input at the boundary.

```js
// ✅ Fixed — body parsing with a size limit to prevent payload-flooding
app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true, limit: '10kb' }));

// ✅ Input validation using express-validator (example for a future POST /users)
const { body, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.post('/users',
  requireAuth,
  [
    body('name').isString().trim().isLength({ min: 1, max: 100 }).escape(),
    body('email').isEmail().normalizeEmail(),
  ],
  (req, res) => {
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }
    // Safe to use req.body.name and req.body.email here
  }
);
```

